# Agent Preferences (global)

These preferences apply to every session, in every project.

## About me
- Andrei Bârsan — senior research scientist at Waabi (autonomous driving simulation)
- Background: PhD (UofT, Raquel Urtasun's group) in CV/deep learning for robotics and
  localization; earlier Uber ATG, ETH (DynSLAM). More: https://siegedog.com/
- Expert: perception, localization, LiDAR, neural rendering, graphics, CV/robotics —
  treat me as senior; skip beginner-level explanations
- General interests: programming, vision, robotics

## Build systems (per project)
- Rust: cargo
- Python: uv / conda
- Bazel: only for complex cross-language projects (not used at home for now)
- Match what the repo uses; if unsure, check for `Cargo.toml`, `pyproject.toml`,
  or `MODULE`/`BUILD` files before assuming a workflow

## Environments
- Run things locally by default; use remote hosts (`wb`, `abarsan-dev-environment`)
  only when I explicitly ask
- The shell tool's zsh wrapper mangles `[[ ... ]]` constructs in one-liner
  commands (they silently vanish / cause bogus bash syntax errors). If a
  bash one-liner with `[[ ]]` fails weirdly, write it to a multi-line script
  file instead — that always works.

## System changes
- For major system tweaks (e.g. enabling zswap, sysctls), ensure they persist
  across reboots (sysctl.d, /etc configs) — not just runtime changes

## My tech stack
- DevOps / infrastructure (CI/CD, containers, Kubernetes, Linux, networking)
- Scripting & automation (bash, Python, Make, CI pipelines)
- Systems & low-level (C/C++, Rust, performance, memory)
- Data / ML (data pipelines, PyTorch, data engineering)
- Computer vision and robotics

## Autonomy
Confirm with me before risky or hard-to-reverse operations, specifically:
- `git push`, force-push, or rewriting shared history
- Deleting files, branches, or data
- Adding, upgrading, or removing dependencies

For everything else, just do it without asking.

## Local LLM (Ollama)
- Model: `qwen3.8:27b` (27B, Q4_K_M) on RTX 3090 24 GB, 125 GiB RAM
- Keep `num_ctx` at ~64k or below: 96k spills the KV cache into system RAM
  and generation drops below 10 tok/s (measured)
- Keep OpenCode's compaction `keep` a few k below `num_ctx`

## OpenCode TUI
- A single accidental `Ctrl-C` must never kill a live session. `cli.json`
  (`~/.config/opencode/cli.json`) overrides `keybinds.app.exit` to
  `["ctrl+d", "<leader>q"]` — `Ctrl-C` is removed from the exit binding.
- OpenCode keybinds have no double-tap/repeat mechanism (only key, press/release,
  `preventDefault`, `fallthrough`), so "double `Ctrl-C` to quit" (Claude Code
  style) isn't expressible in config. Esc (`session.interrupt`) stops a running
  turn; `Ctrl-D` or `Ctrl-X` then `Q` quits. Don't re-add `Ctrl-C` to `app.exit`.

## Reply style
Concise: only what's needed to answer. No filler, no restating the question, no over-explaining.

## Commit messages
Imperative, short subjects (e.g. `fix: handle null pose`), body only when it adds context.

## Definition of done
A code change is done when:
- Tests pass
- Linter and typecheck are clean
- It is committed (or a commit is proposed)
- Docs are kept in sync with the code

## Keep this file current
Treat this file and `opencode.json` as living documents. Whenever a new preference,
convention, or workflow decision emerges in a conversation, proactively update this
file and commit the change so it persists in future sessions.
